<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edict of Potsdam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1685">1685</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a> was issued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick William, the Great Elector</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, in October 1685 in response to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s revocation of the Edict of Nantes. It offered French Huguenot refugees safe settlement, tax exemptions and freedom of worship, and some 20,000 settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a>, many in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, bringing crafts and industries that strengthened the state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edict-of-potsdam.jpg" alt="Edict of Potsdam" width="320" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Der Große Kurfürst Friedrich Wilhelm (The Great Elector) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EdiktPotsdam.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>On 22 October 1685 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> issued the Edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a>, part of a programme of persecution that closed Huguenot churches and schools. It followed the dragonnades, used since 1681 to intimidate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> into converting to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. Estimates of the Protestants who left France over the next two decades range from 210,000 to 900,000. Across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> is estimated to have lost a third of its population through slaughter, famine and plague during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. The western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a> lay within the empire, and the badly depopulated territory faced a desperate labour shortage in the second half of the seventeenth century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Encouraged by the French theologian-philosopher Jakob Abbadie, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick William, Elector of Brandenburg</a>, issued the edict in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a> on 29 October 1685. It provided for Prussian diplomats in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> to ship refugees who had reached Cadzand and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> ports to the Free City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, where Prussian representatives would organise transport up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>. People fleeing eastern and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> were to travel by land via Sedan and the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>, which had become a territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a> in 1666.</p><p>Migrants were promised free choice of settlement, no customs duties or taxes on the assets they brought, and abandoned properties free of debts. Settlers received a six-year tax holiday on property taxes, and farmers on abandoned land a ten-year one. Officials were to hire town houses where families could live rent free for up to four years. Once settled, migrants were to enjoy full legal and civic equality with the elector's subjects, free of any discriminatory droit d'aubaine. They could hold services in French and set up their own schools. On 9 November 1685 translations were printed in German, French and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and sent into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> by secret couriers and local Protestant networks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>About 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> are estimated to have moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a>. The contrast between the edict and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s was widely remarked, and the state became a centre of European immigration that also drew the persecuted of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. The immigrants helped to restore an economy damaged by the wars of the century. The edict also served the elector's interests: a large immigrant community bound to him by religion strengthened him against the landed nobility and powerful Lutheran interests, and it returned land abandoned since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> to use. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uckermark">Uckermark</a> the farmer Jean Harlan of Calais was given an empty farmstead, two horses, a cow and 50 Thalers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edict of Potsdam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Frederick William I and Frederick II settled colonists, among them Huguenots welcomed since the <b>Edict of Potsdam</b> of 1685, drained the Oderbruch marshes, built canals and promoted silk, textiles and the potato.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Great Elector rebuilt by encouraging immigration, above all through the <b>Edict of Potsdam</b> of 1685, which invited some 20,000 Huguenot refugees from France who brought new crafts and industries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
